General elections were held in [[Peru]] on 10 April 2011 to elect the [[President of Peru|president]], the vice presidents, 130 members of [[Congress of the Republic of Peru|Congress]] and five members of the [[Andean Parliament]]. As no presidential candidate received a majority in the first round, a second round was held on 5 June to determine the successor of outgoing president [[Alan García]]. Former army officer [[Ollanta Humala]] narrowly defeated [[Keiko Fujimori]], daughter of imprisoned former President [[Alberto Fujimori]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Bajak|first=Frank|title=Leftist Humala narrowly wins Peru election|agency=Associated Press|work=Forbes|date=6 June 2011|url=https://www.forbes.com/feeds/ap/2011/06/06/general-lt-peru-election_8501624.html|access-date=6 June 2011}}{{dead link|date=January 2022|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> Humala was sworn in as the 94th President of Peru on 28 July.

==Background==
{{more citations needed|date=April 2011}}
After the third presidential term of [[Alberto Fujimori]], new rules were established to curtail presidential authority.{{Clarify|date=April 2011}} The outgoing president is now forbidden to run for reelection until five years have elapsed since the end of a presidential term.

Peruvian politics adhere to a multi-party system, in which no one political group has a majority in Congress. This has led recent administrations to form loose alliances while in office to govern effectively. Such multi-party system has been in place ever since the administration of President [[Alberto Fujimori]] (1990–2000), following his [[1992 Peruvian coup d'état|1992 dissolution of Congress]].

The elections are organised by three groups. First of all the [[RENIEC]] (National Registry of Identification and Civil Status), they are in charge of maintaining the civil records. And by that they define who has to vote and who doesn't. In Peru, all citizens aged 18 to 70 are compelled to vote, elections being discretionary past the age of 70. The real organization of the elections and also of all other referendums is done by the [[ONPE]] (National Office of Electoral Processes). The last organisation is the [[National Jury of Elections|JNE]] (National Jury of Elections), they are looking into the legality of the elections and the campaign plans.

