The election campaigns started early in the summer of 2010. During most of 2010, polls were led by two right wing political parties: National Solidarity, led by former mayor of Lima [[Luis Castañeda Lossio]] and [[Popular Force|Force 2011]], led by ex-president Alberto Fujimori's daughter, [[Keiko Fujimori]]. In November 2010, the [[Alliance for the Great Change]] launched the candidacy of the former prime minister [[Pedro Pablo Kuczynski]]. Claiming a more centrist stand are, the ruling party [[American Popular Revolutionary Alliance|APRA]] and [[Possible Peru]], the latter under the leadership of former Peruvian President [[Alejandro Toledo]].{{Citation needed|date=April 2011}}

The left-wing politician [[Ollanta Humala]] is supported by the [[Peruvian Nationalist Party]].<ref name="aljazresult">{{cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2011/04/201141151929726964.html |title=Leftist ex-soldier leads Peru poll – Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=11 April 2011 |access-date=19 April 2011}}</ref>

The [[Decentralist Social Force Party|Fuerza Social]]<ref>{{cite news|url=http://elcomercio.pe/politica/729545/noticia-manuel-rodriguez-cuadros-renuncio-su-candidatura-presidencial |language=es |title=Manuel Rodríguez Cuadros renunció a su candidatura presidencial |newspaper=El Comercio |date=18 March 2011 |publisher=El Comercio Perú |access-date=19 April 2011}}</ref> and Cambio Radical<ref>{{cite web|url=http://elcomercio.pe/politica/711340/noticia-cambio-radical-presento-al-jeelc-su-lista-candidatos-al-congreso |language=es |title=Cambio Radical presentó al JEELC su lista de candidatos al Congreso|publisher=El Comercio Perú|access-date=19 April 2011}}</ref> do not have presidential candidates. The incumbent [[Alan García]]'s [[American Popular Revolutionary Alliance]]<ref>{{cite web|title=El Apra participaría únicamente en elecciones parlamentarias|url=http://elcomercio.pe/politica/699952/noticia-apra-participaria-unicamente-elecciones-parlamentarias|access-date=19 April 2011|publisher=El Comercio Perú|language=es}}</ref> is also not fielding a candidate in the election.<ref>{{cite web|author=Angel Paez |url=http://english.aljazeera.net/indepth/features/2011/04/20114914395340540.html |title=Run-off expected in Peru election – Features |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=9 April 2011 |access-date=19 April 2011}}</ref>

