[[Alejandro Toledo]] is a former president.

[[Keiko Fujimori]] campaign ran on support of the status quo [[free-market]] policies,<ref name="aljazresult"/> however she was seen as hindered because of her ties to her father Alberto Fujimori,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.soundsandcolours.com/articles/peru/caution-left-turn-only-peruvian-elections/|title=Caution, Left Turn Only – Peruvian Elections|date=22 April 2011|access-date=22 April 2011|archive-date=26 April 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110426155311/http://www.soundsandcolours.com/articles/peru/caution-left-turn-only-peruvian-elections/|url-status=dead}}</ref> who is in prison for corruption and human rights crimes following a crackdown on the [[Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement]] in the 1990s, following the [[Japanese embassy hostage crisis]] in 1996–1997.<ref name="aljazresult"/> In addition, her campaign has been criticized for vote-buying.<ref>[http://wolaperuelections.tumblr.com/post/5868871425/peru-elections-near-a-look-at-the-candidates "Peru Elections Near: A Look at the Candidates"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110717112815/http://wolaperuelections.tumblr.com/post/5868871425/peru-elections-near-a-look-at-the-candidates |date=17 July 2011 }}. WOLA, 26 May 2011. Retrieved 3 June 2011.</ref>

Pedro Pablo Kuczynski, a former prime minister, is also known as "El Gringo" because he has [[United States|U.S. citizenship]]<ref name="diario16.com.pe">{{cite web |url=http://diario16.com.pe/noticia/2514-el-imperio-de-ppk |language=es |title=El imperio de PPK |publisher=Diario16 |access-date=19 April 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110423201311/http://diario16.com.pe/noticia/2514-el-imperio-de-ppk |archive-date=23 April 2011 |url-status=dead}}</ref> and is of European descent. His support was seen as limited outside Lima because of his support amongst the country's elite.

Ollanta Humala, who had once led a military revolt in 2000 that was quickly put down, softened what was seen as his anti-capitalist tone to look more moderate along the lines of Brazil's former president [[Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva]]. He told a campaign rally that "We are willing to make many concessions to unite Peru, we are going to talk with all political forces. Social problems must be resolved through dialogue. Let's vote without fear." Voters were seen to vote against Garcia in order to have a "fairer division of Peru's booming economy – backed by rich mineral resources – a key issue for more than a third of the population still living in poverty." The other candidates tried to discredit him by saying he would increase state control over the economy, roll back reforms and jeopardise about $40bn in potential foreign investment over the next decade in mining and energy exploration. [[Moody's]] ratings agency also said that Peru's investment-grade credit rating would not be threatened should Humala win. Despite this the [[Peruvian nuevo sol|sol]] and the [[Lima Stock Exchange]]'s flagship index main stock index fell over the two weeks before the first round of the election on speculation that Humala would raise mining taxes, increase state subsidies and/or tighten control of such "strategic" sectors as electricity.

Opinion polls conducted in late May 2011 showed Fujimori and Humala in a statistical tie in a runoff vote scheduled for 5 June 2011, with one point separating the two candidates.<ref name="Statistical Tie">[http://wolaperuelections.tumblr.com/post/6071624676/peru-elections-near-a-look-at-the-candidates "Peru Elections Near: A Look at the Candidates"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110717112819/http://wolaperuelections.tumblr.com/post/6071624676/peru-elections-near-a-look-at-the-candidates |date=17 July 2011 }}. WOLA, 1 June 2011. Retrieved 2 June 2011.</ref>{{Better source needed|date=July 2011}}

=== Support for the second round ===

Some of the candidates and parties eliminated in second round have expressed their support for one of the contestants in the run-off election.

''Alliance for the Great Change'' candidate P. P. Kuczynski declared that he would vote for Fujimori,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://noticias.terra.com.pe/elecciones-presidenciales/2011/pedro-pablo-kuczynski-votare-por-keiko-fujimori,92e9fb4537c20310VgnVCM10000098f154d0RCRD.html |title=Pedro Pablo Kuczynski: "Votaré por Keiko Fujimori" |publisher=Terra |date=26 May 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 |archive-date=23 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110823104516/http://noticias.terra.com.pe/elecciones-presidenciales/2011/pedro-pablo-kuczynski-votare-por-keiko-fujimori,92e9fb4537c20310VgnVCM10000098f154d0RCRD.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> as well as his running mate Máximo San Román and ally Humberto Lay (National Restoration), whereas Humanists' leader Yehude Simon declared his support for Humala.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.larepublica.pe/01-06-2011/yehuden-simon-estoy-sorprenido-del-apoyo-de-lay-keiko |title=Yehude Simon: Me sorprende el apoyo de Lay a Keiko |publisher=LaRepublica.pe |date=1 June 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011}}</ref>

The ''Possible Peru'' Alliance and its candidate Alejandro Toledo also announced support for the "Peru Wins"-candidate<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.larepublica.pe/26-05-2011/alejandro-toledo-decide-apoyar-candidatura-de-ollanta-humala |title=Alejandro Toledo decide apoyar candidatura de Ollanta Humala |publisher=LaRepublica.pe |date=26 May 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110630075740/http://www.larepublica.pe/26-05-2011/alejandro-toledo-decide-apoyar-candidatura-de-ollanta-humala |archive-date=30 June 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref> in exchange for a participation in Humala's aspired government.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://elcomercio.pe/politica/755607/noticia-alejandro-toledo-ollanta-humala-me-ofrecio-cogobierno |title=Alejandro Toledo: "Ollanta Humala me ofreció un cogobierno" |publisher=ElComercio.pe |date=11 May 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011}}</ref>

''National Solidarity'' leader Luis Castañeda on the other hand uttered his backing for the Force 2011 frontwoman.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tuteve.tv/noticia/actualidad/luis-castaneda-confirma-votara-keiko-fujimori |title=Luis Castañeda confirma que votará por Keiko Fujimori |publisher=tuteve.tv |date=27 May 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011}}</ref>

''APRA'' did not have a clear party line but former Prime minister [[Javier Velásquez]] inclined to Keiko Fujimori's side.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.larepublica.pe/01-06-2011/fujimori-utiliza-nombre-de-otros-partidos-para-ganar-votos |title=Fujimori utiliza nombre de otros partidos para ganar votos |publisher=LaRepublica.pe |date=21 June 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110604145234/http://www.larepublica.pe/01-06-2011/fujimori-utiliza-nombre-de-otros-partidos-para-ganar-votos |archive-date=4 June 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Nobel laureate writer [[Mario Vargas Llosa]], liberal [[1990 Peruvian general election|presidential candidate of 1990]] and opponent of Alberto Fujimori, stated that he would "never vote" for Fujimori's daughter and warned the nation of a "return to dictatorship" that came up with a victory of Keiko.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.todanoticia.com/26324/vargas-llosa-arremete-contra-keiko/?lang=en |title=Vargas Llosa blasts Keiko Fujimori |publisher=todanoticia.com |date=16 May 2011 |access-date=2 June 2011 |archive-date=17 July 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110717065422/http://www.todanoticia.com/26324/vargas-llosa-arremete-contra-keiko/?lang=en |url-status=dead }}</ref> He, personally, would vote for Humala, "unhappily and with fear" but as the lesser evil.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2011/apr/28/mario-vargas-llosa-peru-election |title=Mario Vargas Llosa under fire for Peru election endorsement |work=The Guardian |location=London |date=28 April 2011 |access-date=2 June 2011  |first=Rory |last=Carroll}}</ref>

