==Reactions==
Fujimori congratulated Humala on his victory after admitting defeat.<ref>[https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-latin-america-13651468 Peru election winner Humala congratulated by rival]. BBC (7 June 2011). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref>

;States
*{{Flag|Bolivia}} – After the first-round, President [[Evo Morales]] sent a letter to Humala congratulating him and said of those who voted for him did so "to advance with the transformation of their nation and its institutions...Receive in my name, from the government and from the Bolivian people, a fraternal and revolutionary salute."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://peripheralrevision.wordpress.com/2011/04/11/bolivias-morales-congratulates-perus-humala-for-1st-round-election-success/ |title=Bolivia's Morales Congratulates Peru's Humala For 1st round election success « Peripheral Revision |publisher=Peripheralrevision.wordpress.com |date=11 April 2011 |access-date=30 July 2011}}</ref>
*{{Flag|Colombia}} – President [[Juan Manuel Santos]] called Humala the next day to congratulate him for his "victory in the electoral vote that consolidates the Peruvian democracy. Santos reiterated Colombia's commitment to continue working at the strengthening of relations between the two countries and of all Latin America."<ref>[http://colombiareports.com/colombia-news/news/16775-santos-congratules-humala-on-winning-peru-elections.html "Santos congratules Humala on winning Peru elections"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110608040027/http://colombiareports.com/colombia-news/news/16775-santos-congratules-humala-on-winning-peru-elections.html |date=8 June 2011 }}. Colombia Reports (6 June 2011). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> This was despite concerns in Colombia of being "surrounded by leftists."<ref>[http://colombiareports.com/opinion/santiago-sosa/16923-what-does-humalas-election-mean-for-colombia.html "What does Humala's election mean for Colombia?"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110720082127/http://colombiareports.com/opinion/santiago-sosa/16923-what-does-humalas-election-mean-for-colombia.html |date=20 July 2011 }}. Colombia Reports (13 June 2011). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref>
:Finance Minister [[Juan Carlos Echeverry (politician)|Juan Carlos Echeverry]] said that a merger between the two countries' stock exchanges was unlikely to be affected. He also said that Humala could "prove the markets wrong" in reference to concerns over former Brazilian President [[Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva]]'s [[2002 Brazilian general election|electoral victory]]. However, the [[Bogotá Stock Exchange]] indices also fell.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Murphy|first1=Helen|date=17 April 2011|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-04-17/humala-victory-won-t-derail-colombia-peru-exchanges-merger-echeverry-says.html|title=Humala Victory Won't Derail Colombia-Peru Exchanges Merger, Echeverry Says|work=Bloomberg|access-date=28 July 2011|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

;Economic
The [[Lima Stock Exchange]] index also plunged the following day<ref>{{cite web |url=http://blog.bitcomet.com/post/1960340/ |title=Nationalist Humala edges out rival in Peru, unnerving investors – 16250762's BLOG |publisher=Blog.bitcomet.com |access-date=29 July 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110810101104/http://blog.bitcomet.com/post/1960340/ |archive-date=10 August 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref> amid concerns of Humala's economic policies and cabinet ministers.

==Government formation==
Former president Alejandro Toledo was set to be in talk with Humala to join his government in some form. However, in mid-July he quit the talks, though he said legislators from their two parties would vote together on some issues.<ref>Faries, Bill. (16 July 2011) [https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-07-16/peru-party-ends-talks-over-humala-government-role-peru-21-says.html Peru Party Ends Talks Over Humala Government Role, Peru.21 Says]. Bloomberg. Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref>

About a week before his inauguration Humala introduced a cabinet line-up that was positively received by financial markets and the business community because most appointees were read as "moderate" and "establishment" figures who thus allayed apparent fears of radical change. His cabinet line-up includes:
