On 18 August, the cabinet authorised the creation of the Ministry of Development and Social Inclusion whose function would be to implement the social programmes of the government to promote "social inclusion." The ministry was created to turn the social programmes into productive tools for the benefit of the poor and that one of its objectives would be to create the conditions so that beneficiaries of the ''Juntos''{{Clarify|date=August 2011}} programme use subsidies for "financial leverage" so as to improve their quality of life.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.andina.com.pe/INgles/Noticia.aspx?id=UAPHQTF/0bg= |title=Peru's Cabinet passes bill to create Development, Social Inclusion Ministry |website=www.andina.com.pe |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319195603/http://www.andina.com.pe/INgles/Noticia.aspx?id=UAPHQTF%2F0bg%3D |archive-date=19 March 2012}}</ref>

As a result of Humala's appointees, which came in contrast to concerns he would introduce changes that could hurt Peru's economic growth, Peru's sol-denominated bonds increased as yields fell from 7.84 percent for maturities in due in August 2020 to 6.12 percent. The bond prices gained 0.29 [[centimo]] to 111.78 centimos per sol, the highest since 23 February.<ref name="bondsRise">{{cite news| url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-08-02/peru-bonds-rise-on-bet-humala-economic-team-to-foster-investment.html | work=Bloomberg | first=John | last=Quigley | title=Peru Bonds Rise on Bet Humala Economic Team to Foster Investment | date=2 August 2011|url-access=subscription}}</ref> Carlos Herrera said that Peru would seek a mining [[windfall profits tax]] after consultations with companies.<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-mining-peru-minister-idUSTRE76L5XH20110722 "Peru's Herrera seeks mining profits tax"]. Reuters. Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref>
<!--possible addition to this section on initiated policies
http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=olfW9uL5AeI= -->

;Reaction
In reaction to the perceived "moderate" appointments the [[Peruvian nuevo sol|sol]] held steady at a three-year high.<ref>[https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-07-25/peru-s-sol-holds-near-three-year-high-after-humala-appointments.html "Peru's Sol Holds Near Three-Year High After Humala Appointments"]. Bloomberg. Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> The credit rating agency [[Fitch Ratings|Fitch]] also reaffirmed Peru's BBB− credit rating with a positive outlook.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://pymnts.com/fitch-affirms-perus-ltfc-idr-at-bbb--outlook-remains-positive-20110727006899/ |title=Fitch Affirms Peru's LTFC IDR at 'BBB-'; Outlook Remains Positive |publisher=pymnts.com |date=13 August 2010 |access-date=29 July 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120714063653/http://pymnts.com/fitch-affirms-perus-ltfc-idr-at-bbb--outlook-remains-positive-20110727006899/ |archive-date=14 July 2012}}</ref> The decision to keep Velarde at the Central Bank also resulted in a rise for the Lima Stock Exchange's indices.<ref>{{cite news|last=Emery |first=Alex |url-access=subscription|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-07-18/peruvian-stocks-surge-after-humala-says-central-bank-president-to-stay.html |title=Peruvian Stocks Surge as Humala Says He'll Keep Velarde Atop Central Bank |publisher=Bloomberg |date=18 July 2011 |access-date=29 July 2011}}</ref> Roque Benavides, the chief executive officer of [[Cia. de Minas Buenaventura SA]], (Peru's largest [[precious metals]] producer) said of Humala's ministerial appointees that "there are very, very good ministers who have been called to contribute. What we have now is better than what we expected."<ref name="bloombergreaction">{{cite news|last1=Murphy |first1=Helen|last2=Quigley|first2=John |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-07-28/humala-backers-cry-foul-as-former-peru-rebel-tilts-to-investors.html |title=Ex-Rebel, Now Peru's President, Riles Backers |publisher=Bloomberg |date=5 June 2011 |access-date=29 July 2011|url-access=subscription}}</ref> Mining companies such as [[Southern Copper]] and Cia. de Minas Buenaventura SA had previously warned against a tax on sales, which could make the mining industry less competitive and affect the US$42 billion in planned mining investment.<ref name="bloombergreaction"/> Southern Copper said that it expected a "positive result" from consultation talks on the proposed new windfall tax levy on mining companies. Financial Planning Manager Raul Jacob said that the government would continue to support new mining projects like the US$1 billion Tia Maria copper mine. "We think the government will establish the taxation but will maintain the {{sic|cost-competiveness|expected=cost-competitiveness}} of the mining industry. [The company is] confident that good investment conditions, stability, social inclusion and growth will prevail in Peru."<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2011-08-02/southern-copper-sees-positive-result-from-talks-with-humala-on-peru-tax.html | work=Bloomberg | first=Alex | last=Emery | title=Southern Copper Sees 'Positive Result' From Talks With Humala on Peru Tax | date=2 August 2011}}</ref>

However, his softening stance on regulating and taxing the mining sector<ref>{{cite web|last=Blas |first=Javier |url=http://www.ft.com/intl/cms/s/0/fb00eb90-b2a7-11e0-bc28-00144feabdc0.html?ftcamp=rss |title=Humala softens stance on Peru's mining sector |work=Financial Times  |date=20 July 2011 |access-date=29 July 2011}}</ref> caused consternation amongst some of his original supporters. Mario Huaman, the head of the [[General Workers' Confederation]] (the largest labour union in Peru, who also endorsed Humala) said that: "We're worried. We'll listen very carefully to what he says in the coming days and see if he shares our views. Then we'll decide our plan of action, our plan to fight. He promised change." Renee Ramirez, the general secretary of the [[Education Workers Union]] said that: "The new government has built up such great hopes that if it doesn't follow through there'll be a big divorce. We're not going to keep quiet. We threw our weight behind Humala but we didn't write him a blank check."

Alvaro Vargas Llosa, a senior fellow at the Washington, D.C.–based [[Independent Institute]] and son of Mario Vargas Llosa said that "He has to find a way to keep the base close while not letting it dictate economic policy." Erasto Almeida, a political analyst at [[Eurasia Group]] added that if his opinion rating drops further or the economy slows that Humala "could be tempted later on in his mandate" to adopt less market-friendly policies.<ref name="bloombergreaction"/>

Indigenous groups, such as AIDESEP in the Amazon, have expressed disappointment with Humala's move away from the left amidst his campaign promises to champion the [[Inca]] empire. They cited such reasons as Humala plan to go through with a Garcia-era proposal to bring in up to US$20 billion in the next five years, US$6.2 billion of which Garcia had already lined-up, for such measures as [[oil exploration]] in the [[Amazon Basin|Amazon]]. Alberto Pizango, the head AIDESEP, said that "The [indigenous] communities had entrusted this government to oversee a real, profound change. But Humala has altered his discourse, leading the people to say this government will just be more of the same." He also criticised the appointment of Herrera because of the latter's approval of petroleum concessions when he first held the ministerial post in 2000.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-peru-humala-indigenous-idUSTRE77G5RU20110817 | work=Reuters | title=Analysis: Peru's indigenous losing faith in reformed Humala | date=17 August 2011}}</ref>

Former President Alan García chaired his final cabinet meeting on 27 July.<ref>[http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=478TtQy5taM= President Garcia chairs last Cabinet meeting today] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319195235/http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=478TtQy5taM= |date=19 March 2012 }} ANDINA (Peru News Agency). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> At it he said:<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.peruthisweek.com/news-224-Perus-Garcia-achievements-will-be-recognized-by-history/ |title=Peru's Garcia: Achievements Will Be Recognized By History |publisher=Peruthisweek.com |access-date=29 July 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120325003024/http://www.peruthisweek.com/news-224-Perus-Garcia-achievements-will-be-recognized-by-history/ |archive-date=25 March 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref>
<blockquote>I wanted to thank all of the ministers, also the regional presidents have put in a great deal of effort into their jobs, and mayors who are those who develop directly and closely with the people. [I am convinced that] when you put zeal aside, history will recognise what these ministers achieved with their effort. Much will have to be done for our country, but I think that the path chosen was the correct one, and the perseverance and the dedication was necessary. A salute to Peru, to the youth of Peru, to the poor of Peru, to the authorities of Peru, and as a follower of [[Haya de La Torre]] all I wanted to do was make a government that gives bread with freedom. A salute to Peru, to its future and success."</blockquote>

===Inauguration===
Prior to his inauguration on 28 July, Humala's popularity fell in opinion polls after he suspended his brother [[Alexis Humala]] from Gana Peru following allegations of corruption in relation to deals with [[Gazprom]]. His approval rating of 70% fell to 41%.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20111005025344/http://af.reuters.com/article/commoditiesNews/idAFN1E76G06020110717 "Peru's Humala falls in poll before inauguration"] Reuters. Af.reuters.com (17 July 2011). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref>

For inauguration day itself Lima's main downtown square was scheduled to be open without security restrictions. Javier Sanguinetti, police chief for the Lima region, said that his office was working with the tourism police department and that 10,000 police officers would be deployed at popular tourist attractions around Lima on 28 and 29 July.<ref>[http://www.perucanadatrade.com/index.php/projects-new/456-limas-main-square-to-open-to-visitors-during-humala-inauguration "Lima's main square to open to visitors during Humala inauguration"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120328052410/http://www.perucanadatrade.com/index.php/projects-new/456-limas-main-square-to-open-to-visitors-during-humala-inauguration |date=28 March 2012 }}. Perucanadatrade.com. Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref>

Former President Alan García was not scheduled to attend the event because, according to him, the inauguration "is to hear the new president's message" and that although he attended [[Alberto Fujimori]]'s inauguration in 1990 "there was such unrest in congress, because of my presence, that I'd rather focus on my health, and say ‘Let the new president give his message freely, let's not vent our frustrations in front of foreign heads of state.'" His critics read this as a snub to Humala.<ref>[http://www.livinginperu.com/news/15572 "Peru's García will not attend Humala's inauguration"]. Living in Peru. Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> However, [[Foreign policy of Ollanta Humala|foreign dignitaries]] such as Colombia's Juan Manuel Santos was scheduled to attend the event,<ref>[http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?id=J+RtcQN7wqE= Colombian president to attend CAN Summit, Humala's inauguration] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319195053/http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?id=J+RtcQN7wqE= |date=19 March 2012 }}. ANDINA (20 July 2011). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> along with Brazil's [[Dilma Rousseff]],<ref>[http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?id=F0dgngkR67k= Brazilian president to attend Humala's inauguration] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319195314/http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?id=F0dgngkR67k= |date=19 March 2012 }}. ANDINA. Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> Uruguay's [[José Mujica]],<ref>[http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=G/HcvHbIIAs= Uruguayan president to attend Humala's inauguration] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319195356/http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=G%2FHcvHbIIAs%3D |date=19 March 2012 }}. ANDINA. Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> Panama's [[Ricardo Martinelli]]<ref name="statement">{{cite web |url=http://www.livinginperu.com/news-235-Heads-of-state-arrive-to-Peru-for-Humala-inauguration/ |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130128020150/http://www.livinginperu.com/news-235-Heads-of-state-arrive-to-Peru-for-Humala-inauguration/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=28 January 2013 |title=Heads Of State Arrive To Peru For Humala Inauguration |publisher=Livinginperu.com |access-date=29 July 2011 }}</ref> (and his wife First Lady [[Marta Linares de Martinelli]]),<ref>{{cite web |author=Don Winner |url=http://www.panama-guide.com/article.php/20110727150743687 |title=Martinelli Traveling To Peru To Attend Ollanta Humala's Inauguration |publisher=Panama Guide |access-date=30 July 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110811115134/http://www.panama-guide.com/article.php/20110727150743687 |archive-date=11 August 2011 |url-status=usurped }}</ref> Argentina's [[Cristina Fernández de Kirchner]], Chile's [[Sebastián Piñera]], Ecuador's [[Rafael Correa]],<ref name="voaguests"/> Bolivia's Evo Morales, Guatemala's [[Álvaro Colom]], Honduras’ [[Porfirio Lobo]],<ref>[http://coa.counciloftheamericas.org/articles/3526/Ollanta_Humala_Takes_Presidential_Helm_in_Peru/ Ollanta Humala Takes Presidential Helm in Peru] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111004183908/http://coa.counciloftheamericas.org/articles/3526/Ollanta_Humala_Takes_Presidential_Helm_in_Peru/ |date=4 October 2011 }}. Coa.counciloftheamericas.org (27 July 2011). Retrieved 31 July 2011.</ref> South Africa's [[Jacob Zuma]]<ref>[http://www.iol.co.za/news/politics/zuma-inauguration-guest-list-1.441552 Zuma inauguration guest list – Politics | IOL News]. IOL.co.za (27 April 2009). Retrieved 31 July 2011.</ref> and Georgia's [[Mikheil Saakashvili]].<ref>[http://pik.tv/en/news/story/15682-president-saakashvili-attends-inauguration-in-peru President Saakashvili Attends Inauguration in Peru – Kanal PIK TV] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120324122324/http://pik.tv/en/news/story/15682-president-saakashvili-attends-inauguration-in-peru |date=24 March 2012 }}. Pik.tv. Retrieved 31 July 2011.</ref> They would also be joined by Cuban First Vice President [[Jose Ramon Machado Ventura]] and his delegation,<ref>[http://www.plenglish.com/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=310119&Itemid=1 Prensa Latina News Agency – Cuban Vice President to Attend Ollanta Humala Inauguration]. Plenglish.com (1 January 1970). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> Spain's [[crown prince]] [[Felipe, Prince of Asturias]],<ref name="perutimes">[http://www.peruviantimes.com/29/ollanta-humala-inaugurated-as-perus-president/13223/ Ollanta Humala inaugurated as Peru's president]. ''Peruvian Times'' (22 February 1999). Retrieved 31 July 2011.</ref> Secretary-general of the [[Organization of American States]] [[Jose Miguel Insulza]], the U.S. ambassador,<ref name="voaguests">{{cite web|url=http://blogs.voanews.com/breaking-news/2011/07/28/peru-swears-in-humala-as-president/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120315185607/http://blogs.voanews.com/breaking-news/2011/07/28/peru-swears-in-humala-as-president/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=15 March 2012 |title=Peru Swears In Humala as President « VOA Breaking News |publisher=Voice of America |date=20 August 2009 |access-date=29 July 2011}}</ref> the brother of South Korean President [[Lee Myung-bak]], [[Lee Sang-deuk]] of the incumbent [[Grand National Party]]<ref>[http://www.livinginperu.com/news/15535 News » South Korean president's envoy to attend Humala inauguration]. Living in Peru (19 July 2011). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref> and Chinese President [[Hu Jintao]]'s special envoy Minister of Agriculture [[Han Changfu]].<ref>[http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?id=6sWUxpLivBE= Chinese envoy to attend Humala's inauguration] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319195124/http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?id=6sWUxpLivBE= |date=19 March 2012 }}. ANDINA (22 July 2011). Retrieved 28 July 2011.</ref>

Piñera, who was welcomed by outgoing Economics Minister Ismael Benavides<ref name="statement"/> after his arrival at 23:00<ref name="cl"/> said: "From the core of my soul, I want to wish President Humala the best of luck, as well as to the Peruvian government and the Peruvian people." Santos, who was welcomed by outgoing Prime Minister [[Rosario Fernández]], said that "We consider Peru a true strategic partner on many fronts. Every relationship can be strengthened, improved. The relationship with Peru has been extraordinary, but we're going to see how we can continue feeding it, strengthening it, improving it."<ref name="statement"/> Machado Ventura, who was welcomed by the outgoing Minister of the Interior Miguel Hidalgo, relayed greetings and said that Humala electoral victory was an "example of the continuing advance of progressive forces in Latin America;" he added that the proposed a "nationalist programme" would "promote greater equity in the distribution of the country's wealth and that Cuba wishes him success in this effort." His delegation also included Foreign Minister [[Bruno Rodríguez Parrilla]], Deputy Foreign Minister Rogelio Sierra, the new Cuban ambassador to Peru Juana Martínez González and the outgoing Cuban ambassador Luis Delfín Pérez. The delegation was also invited Peru's commemoration of their 190 anniversary of independence.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.granma.cu/ingles/ouramerica-i/28jul-Machado-Ventura.html |title=Machado Ventura arrives in Peru to attend Humala inauguration |publisher=granma.cu |access-date=29 July 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111130084905/http://granma.cu/ingles/ouramerica-i/28jul-Machado-Ventura.html |archive-date=30 November 2011}}</ref> The outgoing President Alan García hosted a reception for the visiting heads of state and foreign dignitaries the night before the inauguration.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.peruthisweek.com/news-235-Heads-of-state-arrive-to-Peru-for-Humala-inauguration/ |title=Heads of State Arrive To Peru For Humala Inauguration |publisher=Peruthisweek.com |access-date=29 July 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120123151728/http://www.peruthisweek.com/news-235-Heads-of-state-arrive-to-Peru-for-Humala-inauguration/ |archive-date=23 January 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Morales and Piñera also scheduled a [[Chile-Bolivia relations|bilateral meeting]] on the sidelines of the event.<ref name="cl">Ebergenyi, Ivan. (27 July 2011) [http://www.santiagotimes.cl/world/latin-america/22079-chiles-president-expected-to-meet-with-evo-morales- Chile's president expected to meet with Evo Morales]. Santiagotimes.cl. Retrieved 31 July 2011.</ref>

Garcia left the presidential palace after he passed on the presidential sash to Humala.<ref name="inaug"/> Before wearing the sash, Humala also made a speech in which he promised "to fight for social integration, particularly of the poorest."<ref name="google1"/> He also said that he would not alter the existing market-oriented policies and would keep trade policies intact; he added that he provide a minimum pension for all Peruvians over the age of 65 and raise the minimum wage. "We want the term 'social exclusion' to disappear from our language and lives forever. Economic growth and social inclusion will march together." In tackling social conflict and drug trafficking he said that the United States is a "strategic partner."<ref name="inaug"/> He also quoted South Africa's [[Nelson Mandela]] when he argued that there cannot be a democracy where misery and "social asymmetry" persist."<ref name="aljaz">{{cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2011/07/201172994611161939.html |title=Peru's new president: 'poor are my priority' – Americas |publisher=Al Jazeera English |access-date=29 July 2011}}</ref> However, he broke with tradition when he did not travel to Congress to attend the inauguration.<ref name="inaug">{{cite news|author=Dan Collyns |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-latin-america-14321996 |title=BBC News – Ollanta Humala is sworn in as new Peru president |publisher=BBC  |access-date=29 July 2011 |date=28 July 2011}}</ref> During the swearing-in ceremony he was shouted down by the "Fujimoristas" (supporters of Keiko Fujimori and her father Alberto) after he said that was taking power" in the spirit of the [[Constitution of Peru|1979 constitution]]:<ref name="aljaz"/> "The constitution of 1979, the last constitution of democratic origin, which many have not respected and that's why it has been forgotten, is for me a true inspiration for its national and democratic content."<ref name="perutimes"/>

===Initial initiatives===
On 25 August, the Congress gave its vote of confidence in approving the Council of Ministers after Prime Minister Salomon Lerner Ghitis spoke to the Congress by a vote of 90 in favour, zero against and 33 abstentations, all of whom were from Fuerza 2011.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.peruviantimes.com/26/congress-approves-humalas-first-cabinet-following-speech-by-lerner-ghitis/13500/#more-13500|title=Congress approves Humala's first cabinet following speech by Lerner Ghitis|date=26 August 2011 |access-date=21 April 2016}}</ref> Tejada expressed his satisfaction and gratitude at the recognition of the "technical support" for the government's programmes and goals. "We call on Peruvians to be confident that President Ollanta Humala is setting the beginning of a new phase of growth with social inclusion in the history of Peru, respecting human rights and freedom of speech."<ref name="health">{{Cite web |url=http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=80%2FI+aGGZ5I%3D |title = Peru begins new phase in history: growth with social inclusion  |access-date=3 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319195454/http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=80%2FI+aGGZ5I%3D |archive-date=19 March 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The initiatives were backed by the [[National Confederation of Private Business Associations]] (CONFIEP) as confirmed by President Humberto Speziani who said that "Overall we agree with all ten policies announced by the Cabinet, which is headed by Salomon Lerner. [Ghitis' goals are] "necessary to grow with social inclusion."<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=%2Fh2muxBY58M%3D |title = Entrepreneurs back Peru's government policy announced by Cabinet  |access-date=3 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319195542/http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?Id=%2Fh2muxBY58M%3D |archive-date=19 March 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

One of Humala's first tasks amongst his promises for social inclusion was to start the implementation of a two-stage 25% increase in the monthly minimum wage to 750 sol and unveiling a pension increase for those older than 65.<ref>[http://www.laht.com/article.asp?ArticleId=411539&CategoryId=10718 Latin American Herald Tribune – Peru Inaugurates Humala – Garcia and Constitution "No Shows"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120610163709/http://www.laht.com/article.asp?ArticleId=411539&CategoryId=10718 |date=10 June 2012 }}. Laht.com. Retrieved 31 July 2011.</ref>

<!--Interior Mins suspension of cocoa eradication programme and US reaction-->
Humala also sought to create a national flag carrier airline in cooperation with private investment, especially to such unprofitable domestic markets as the hinterlands of the [[Andes]] and the Amazon.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://blogs.ft.com/beyond-brics/2011/08/19/peru-an-airline-at-all-loss/#ixzz1W54VlVkB|title=Peru: an airline at all losses|work=Financial Times|access-date=21 April 2016}}</ref>

During the first month of Humala's presidency, an agreement was reached with mining companies that would increase taxes by up to three billion sols a year however, Carlos Herrera said a new royalty rate had not been set as yet. Prime Minister Salomon Lerner Ghitis said that "this tax will not affect investment or companies' competitiveness."<ref>{{cite news| url=https://af.reuters.com/article/commoditiesNews/idAFN1E77O0WQ20110825 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120927072433/http://af.reuters.com/article/commoditiesNews/idAFN1E77O0WQ20110825 | url-status=dead | archive-date=27 September 2012 | work=Reuters | title=UPDATE 4-Peru gov't, miners reach deal to increase royalties | date=25 August 2011}}</ref>

Amongst his initial social policies, modeled after Brazil during the tenure of Lula da Silva, were: a non-contributory basic pension of about US$90 per month for the elderly who lack other provisions, a state-run child-care programme, an increase in scholarships for poor students who want a university education and the expansion of a [[conditional cash transfer]] scheme for the poor. However, he said that such programmes would be introduced gradually, hence, according to government officials, the cost for the following year would be a more moderate figure of US$275m.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.economist.com/the-americas/2011/08/06/the-brazilian-way | newspaper=The Economist | title=The Brazilian way | date=6 August 2011}}</ref>

